          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following correctly describes the classification of ribs eleven and twelve?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are true ribs with costal cartilage attaching directly to the sternum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ribs 1–7 are the true ribs (vertebrosternal). Their costal cartilage connects directly to the sternum. Ribs 11 and 12 have no sternal attachment at all.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are false ribs whose costal cartilage joins the cartilage of the rib above</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ribs 8–10 are the false ribs (vertebrochondral). Their cartilage connects to the cartilage of the rib above. Ribs 11 and 12 lack even this indirect sternal connection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are floating ribs with free anterior ends and no sternal connection</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs — they have no anterior attachment to the sternum or to other costal cartilages, leaving their anterior ends free.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are accessory ribs that arise from the seventh cervical vertebra</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A cervical rib is a separate anomaly arising from C7. It is not the same as ribs 11 and 12, which are normal floating ribs articulating with thoracic vertebrae.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The three groups are: true ribs (1–7, direct sternal attachment), false ribs (8–10, indirect via cartilage of the rib above), and floating ribs (11–12, free anterior end)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs — their anterior ends are free, with no sternal or cartilaginous connection."
                }
              ],
