          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Rib notching, characterised by scalloping along the inferior borders of the ribs</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Rib notching, characterised by scalloping along the inferior borders of the ribs on a chest X-ray, is a classic sign of which condition?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cervical rib causing thoracic outlet syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A cervical rib (from C7) compresses the brachial plexus and subclavian vessels but does not cause rib notching. Rib notching is a vascular erosion phenomenon, not a compression syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pectus carinatum associated with Marfan syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pectus carinatum (pigeon chest) is a sternal protrusion deformity associated with Marfan syndrome. It does not involve the rib borders and does not produce notching on X-ray.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coarctation of the aorta</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! In coarctation of the aorta, the narrowed aortic segment forces blood through dilated collateral intercostal arteries, which gradually erode the inferior rib borders — producing the characteristic notching seen on chest X-ray.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pectus excavatum compressing the heart</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pectus excavatum (funnel chest) involves depression of the sternum and may compromise cardiopulmonary function, but it does not erode the rib borders or produce notching.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Rib notching is classically associated with coarctation of the aorta. The narrowed aorta forces blood through collateral intercostal arteries, which enlarge and erode the undersurface of the ribs over time."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Rib notching is a hallmark radiological sign of coarctation of the aorta, caused by enlarged collateral intercostal arteries eroding the inferior rib margins."
                }
              ],
